---
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import TopicClusterNav from '../components/TopicClusterNav.astro';

const pageTitle = 'Angular vs Rounded Rock: Choose for the Project';
const metaDescription = 'Compare how stone shape, grading and the prepared base affect walking, driving and drainage before estimating your order.';

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'Why does angular rock lock in place better than rounded rock?',
    a: 'Fractured faces on angular crushed stone create mechanical friction between adjacent particles, whereas smooth rounded gravel and river rock tend to roll under shear forces from tires or shoes.'
  },
  {
    q: 'Can rounded pea gravel or river rock be compacted for a firm driveway?',
    a: 'Vibratory tamping settles loose particles slightly, but rounded stones without fractured interlocking edges and binding fines will continue shifting under turning wheels or narrow chair legs.'
  },
  {
    q: 'Does stone shape change the cubic yards needed at a fixed depth?',
    a: 'No. Volume comes from area multiplied by depth (Area × Depth ÷ 324). Stone shape and size grading affect your chosen finished depth and supplier weight per cubic yard, not geometric volume.'
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={pageTitle} description={metaDescription} canonicalPath="/angular-vs-rounded-rock">
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'Article',
      headline: 'Angular vs Rounded Rock: Match Shape to Use',
      description: metaDescription,
      mainEntityOfPage: 'https://rockcoveragecalculator.com/angular-vs-rounded-rock',
      inLanguage: 'en',
      dateModified: '2026-11-25'
    })} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'FAQPage',
      mainEntity: faqItems.map(item => ({
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: item.q,
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a }
      }))
    })} />
  </Fragment>

  <Header />
  <main id="main-content" class="container practical-guide">
    <Breadcrumbs />
    <h1>Angular vs Rounded Rock: Match Shape to Use</h1>
    <p class="intro">Compare angular and rounded particles together with grading, fines and confinement. Shape affects movement, but it does not by itself establish drainage, load capacity or the correct layer specification.</p>

    <h2>Shape is one part of the choice</h2>
    <p>Angular rock has fractured faces; rounded rock has smoother edges. Angular particles can resist movement by interlocking. Rounded pebbles tend to move under feet and wheels. These descriptions do not establish a material’s strength, drainage rate or friction angle: size distribution, fines, moisture, confinement and the ground underneath also matter.</p>

    <h2>Match the product to its layer</h2>
    <h3>Driveways and loaded surfaces</h3>
    <p>Distinguish the base from the wearing layer. A specified dense-graded base contains a range of particle sizes and fines that can compact. Clean, open-graded angular stone is a different product and should not be substituted solely because both are called gravel. Rounded decorative rock can move under turning tires; use the aggregate and layer thickness required by the driveway plan.</p>
    <h3>Paths and seating areas</h3>
    <p>Small rounded pea gravel may feel smoother than sharp crushed stone, but a loose surface can be difficult for chair legs, wheelchairs and other small wheels. A thicker loose layer may increase movement. Try an actual sample with the intended footwear or furniture. A firm base and secure border help containment; the material name alone does not establish accessibility or slip safety.</p>
    <h3>Drainage layers</h3>
    <p>Both angular and rounded products can be supplied in clean drainage gradings. Ask for the specified particle size and fines content. A #57 size designation does not by itself guarantee washed stone or a particular void percentage. A drain also needs an appropriate outlet, pipe and soil-separation detail. Stone quantity does not establish hydraulic capacity.</p>

    <h2>Can rounded stone be compacted?</h2>
    <p>Placement can rearrange and settle particles, but smooth stone does not form the same interlocking surface as a suitable angular base. Follow the product and installation specification for equipment, lift thickness and confinement. Avoid a universal rule that every rounded product can never be vibrated or that every angular product becomes rigid after tamping.</p>

    <h2>Estimate volume before converting weight</h2>
    <p>For a 10 × 20 ft bed at 3 in, the volume is 200 × 3 ÷ 324 = 1.85 cubic yards before extra material. Shape does not change that geometry. Your chosen product’s loose bulk weight changes the tonnage; a different layer depth changes both volume and weight. The <a href="https://dirtandgravel.psu.edu/general-resources/dglvr-materials-calculator/">Penn State materials calculator</a> distinguishes shipped loose material from finished depth.</p>

    <h2>What to ask the supplier</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>What is the actual product, grading, particle shape and fines content?</li>
      <li>Is the quote for loose cubic yards, short tons, or volume-labeled bags?</li>
      <li>What loose bulk weight and placement allowance apply to this product?</li>
      <li>What base, containment and maintenance will the intended use need?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Continue with the <a href="/which-rock-should-i-use">material selection helper</a>, <a href="/crushed-stone-calculator">crushed stone calculator</a>, or <a href="/pea-gravel-calculator">pea gravel calculator</a>.</p>

    <section class="faq-block" aria-labelledby="shape-faq-heading">
      <h2 id="shape-faq-heading">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      {faqItems.map(item => <details><summary>{item.q}</summary><p>{item.a}</p></details>)}
    </section>

    <TopicClusterNav />
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .practical-guide { max-width: 900px; padding-block: var(--space-10); }
  h1 { font-size: var(--text-3xl); margin-block: var(--space-5); color: var(--text-primary); }
  h2 { font-size: var(--text-xl); margin-block: var(--space-7) var(--space-3); color: var(--text-primary); }
  h3 { font-size: var(--text-lg); margin-block: var(--space-5) var(--space-2); color: var(--text-primary); }
  p, li { line-height: 1.75; color: var(--text-secondary); }
  p, ul, ol { margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  a { color: var(--text-primary); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px; }
  ul, ol { padding-left: 1.4rem; }
  .faq-block details { background: var(--bg-card); border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: var(--radius-md); padding: var(--space-4); margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .faq-block summary { cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; color: var(--text-primary); }
  .faq-block details p { margin: var(--space-3) 0 0; }
</style>
